/-
# Groups of finite width and the minimal condition (Kourovka Notebook Problem 1.81)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 1.81 (L. N. Shevrin): the width of a group
`G` is the least cardinal `m` such that every subgroup generated by a finite set `S` is generated by
a subset of `S` of cardinality at most `m`. (a) Does a group of finite width satisfy the minimal
condition for subgroups? (b) Does a group with the minimal condition for subgroups have finite
width? (c) The same questions for locally finite groups; in particular, is a locally finite group of
finite width a Chernikov group?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Index

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.FiniteWidthMinimalConditionKourovka1Problem81Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 1.81 (L. N.
Shevrin). UnsolvedMath record UM-00700070 (`KOU-1.81`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

Finite width is the defining property for some `m : ℕ`. The minimal condition is `WellFoundedLT
(Subgroup G)`. Part (c) is formalized as its "in particular" question, the one the Archive answers.
A Chernikov group is read as a group with an abelian subgroup of finite index satisfying the minimal
condition; abelian groups with the minimal condition are exactly the direct products of finitely
many quasicyclic groups and a finite group, so this is the usual definition.
-/

/-- `G` has finite width (L. N. Shevrin): for some `m : ℕ`, every subgroup generated by a finite set
`S` is generated by a subset of `S` with at most `m` elements. -/
def HasFiniteWidth (G : Type*) [Group G] : Prop :=
  ∃ m : ℕ, ∀ S : Finset G, ∃ T ⊆ S, T.card ≤ m ∧
    Subgroup.closure (T : Set G) = Subgroup.closure (S : Set G)

/-- `G` is locally finite: every finitely generated subgroup is finite. -/
def IsLocallyFinite (G : Type*) [Group G] : Prop :=
  ∀ S : Finset G, (Subgroup.closure (S : Set G) : Set G).Finite

/-- `G` is a Chernikov group: it has an abelian subgroup of finite index satisfying the minimal
condition on subgroups. An abelian group with the minimal condition is a direct product of finitely
many quasicyclic groups and a finite group, so this is the usual definition (a finite extension of
a direct product of finitely many quasicyclic groups). -/
def IsChernikov (G : Type*) [Group G] : Prop :=
  ∃ A : Subgroup G, A.FiniteIndex ∧ (∀ x ∈ A, ∀ y ∈ A, x * y = y * x) ∧ WellFoundedLT (Subgroup A)

/-- Kourovka 1.81(a) (L. N. Shevrin): every group of finite width satisfies the minimal condition
on subgroups. -/
def finiteWidthMinimalCondition : Prop :=
  ∀ (G : Type) [Group G], HasFiniteWidth G → WellFoundedLT (Subgroup G)

/-- Kourovka 1.81(b): every group with the minimal condition on subgroups has finite width. -/
def minimalConditionFiniteWidth : Prop :=
  ∀ (G : Type) [Group G], WellFoundedLT (Subgroup G) → HasFiniteWidth G

/-- Kourovka 1.81(c): every locally finite group of finite width is a Chernikov group. -/
def locallyFiniteFiniteWidthChernikov : Prop :=
  ∀ (G : Type) [Group G], IsLocallyFinite G → HasFiniteWidth G → IsChernikov G

/--
Resolved false: the Kourovka Archive answers 1.81(a) "Not always", citing Ivanov's candidate
dissertation (1988): some group of finite width does not satisfy the minimal condition for
subgroups. Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The
Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 1.81
(L. N. Shevrin); resolution: S. V. Ivanov, Geometric methods in the study of groups with given
subgroup properties, Cand. Diss., Moscow Univ., 1988 (a); G. S. Deryabina, Math. USSR-Sb. 52 (1985),
481-490 (b); V. P. Shunkov, Algebra and Logic 9 (1970), 137-151 and 10 (1971), 127-142 (c), Kourovka
Archive, Problem 1.81.
-/
public theorem_wanted finiteWidthMinimalCondition_refuted : ¬ finiteWidthMinimalCondition

/--
Resolved false: the Kourovka Archive answers 1.81(b) "Not always", citing Deryabina (1985): some
group with the minimal condition for subgroups has infinite width. Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D.
Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue,
arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 1.81 (L. N. Shevrin); resolution: S.
V. Ivanov, Geometric methods in the study of groups with given subgroup properties, Cand. Diss.,
Moscow Univ., 1988 (a); G. S. Deryabina, Math. USSR-Sb. 52 (1985), 481-490 (b); V. P. Shunkov,
Algebra and Logic 9 (1970), 137-151 and 10 (1971), 127-142 (c), Kourovka Archive, Problem 1.81.
-/
public theorem_wanted minimalConditionFiniteWidth_refuted : ¬ minimalConditionFiniteWidth

/--
Resolved true: the Kourovka Archive answers 1.81(c) "Yes, it is", citing Shunkov (1970, 1971): every
locally finite group of finite width is a Chernikov group. Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov
(eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49
(2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 1.81 (L. N. Shevrin); resolution: S. V. Ivanov,
Geometric methods in the study of groups with given subgroup properties, Cand. Diss., Moscow Univ.,
1988 (a); G. S. Deryabina, Math. USSR-Sb. 52 (1985), 481-490 (b); V. P. Shunkov, Algebra and Logic 9
(1970), 137-151 and 10 (1971), 127-142 (c), Kourovka Archive, Problem 1.81.
-/
public theorem_wanted locallyFiniteFiniteWidthChernikov_holds : locallyFiniteFiniteWidthChernikov

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.FiniteWidthMinimalConditionKourovka1Problem81Wanted
